":"You can find beta-eudesmol in familiar culinary and aromatic plants like ginger (Zingiber officinale), bay leaf (Laurus nobilis), and clary sage (Salvia sclarea), as well as in the traditional herb Atractylodes lancea.","why_researchers_care":"Researchers are drawn to beta-eudesmol for its intriguing interactions with cellular signaling pathways, particularly regarding cell proliferation and apoptosis. The scientific community is actively exploring its potential in cellular health and oncology, supported by early human trials (Oxford level 1b, individual randomised trial). There is also keen interest in how this molecule might influence fluid metabolism and allergic responses at the cellular level.","structure_function":[{"claim_id":"cancer","statement":"May support cellular health and is being studied for its role in regulating cell proliferation.","evidence_level":"human_trials","basis":"This rests on 21 papers, including an individual randomised human trial and 18 observational studies.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"1b","label":"individual randomised trial"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"rct","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":1},{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":18},{"design":"review","eco":"ECO:0000033","papers":1}],"paper_count":21},{"claim_id":"antioxidant","statement":"Is being studied for its potential to support the body's natural antioxidant defenses.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"This is based on 15 papers, including 4 human observational studies and several animal and lab experiments.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":4},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":4},{"design":"in_vitro","eco":"ECO:0000181","papers":3},{"design":"review","eco":"ECO:0000033","papers":1}],"paper_count":15},{"claim_id":"anti-inflammatory","statement":"Early work points toward a role in supporting healthy inflammatory and allergic responses.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"This is supported by 7 papers, including human observational research and animal models.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":2},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":3},{"design":"in_vitro","eco":"ECO:0000181","papers":2}],"paper_count":7},{"claim_id":"antimicrobial","statement":"May support natural antimicrobial defenses.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"This rests on 13 papers, primarily human observational and in vitro studies.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":6},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":1},{"design":"in_vitro","eco":"ECO:0000181","papers":5}],"paper_count":13},{"claim_id":"insect","statement":"Is being explored as a natural insect repellent.","evidence_level":"animal","basis":"This is based on 5 animal and bench studies.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0007120","label":"animal model system study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"bench or animal research"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":5}],"paper_count":5}],"hypothesized_benefits":[{"claim_id":"cancer","area":"Cellular Health and Oncology","hypothesis":"Researchers are exploring whether beta-eudesmol can inhibit tumor cell proliferation and induce apoptosis via specific signaling pathways, such as MAPK and FGFR. If this bears out, it could become a valuable complementary compound in cellular therapies.","evidence_level":"human_trials","paper_count":21,"study_to_test_it":"A multi-center, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled Phase II trial in patients with specific solid tumors, measuring tumor response rates, progression-free survival, and apoptotic biomarkers over 12 months.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"1b","label":"individual randomised trial"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"rct","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":1},{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":18},{"design":"review","eco":"ECO:0000033","papers":1}]},{"claim_id":"anti-inflammatory","area":"Allergy and Immune Response","hypothesis":"Early animal work points toward beta-eudesmol's ability to inhibit mast cell degranulation, raising the exciting question of whether it could support respiratory or systemic allergy relief in humans.","evidence_level":"human_observational","paper_count":7,"study_to_test_it":"A randomized, placebo-controlled human trial measuring serum histamine levels, inflammatory biomarkers, and self-reported allergy symptom scores over a 4-week period during peak pollen season.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":2},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":3},{"design":"in_vitro","eco":"ECO:0000181","papers":2}]},{"claim_id":"digestive","area":"Fluid Metabolism and Digestion","hypothesis":"Researchers are exploring whether beta-eudesmol's interaction with the cAMP-AQP2 pathway can promote healthy fluid metabolism and digestive function.","evidence_level":"animal","paper_count":2,"study_to_test_it":"A first-in-human crossover study measuring urine osmolality, aquaporin-2 excretion, and gastrointestinal transit time in healthy volunteers over 14 days.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0007120","label":"animal model system study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"bench or animal research"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"animal","eco